    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d503">

                <pb facs="0852" n="732" sameAs="frus:frus1922v01/pg732"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">846g.113/—</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Secretary of State to
                        the Secretary of the Treasury</hi> (<hi
                            rendition="#italic">Mellon</hi>)</head>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Washington</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1922-06-24"><hi rendition="#italic">June 24,
                        1922</hi></date>.</dateline></opener>
                <p><hi rendition="#small-caps">Sir</hi>: I have the honor to acknowledge the receipt
                    of Mr. Dover’s letter of June 10, 1922,<anchor n="68" xml:id="d503-f1"/>
                    inquiring whether permits are required for <pb facs="0853" n="733"
                        xml:id="pg733"/> shipments of arms or munitions of war to Hongkong,
                    Kwantung, Korea and French Indo-China.</p>
                <p>In reply you are advised that permits are necessary for shipments to the leased
                    territory of Kwantung, but are not required for shipments to the British Crown
                    colony of Hongkong, Korea or French Indo-China.</p>
                <p>I have [etc.]</p>
                <closer>For the Secretary of State:<lb/><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Leland
                            Harrison</hi></signed><lb/><hi rendition="#italic">Assistant
                        Secretary</hi></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d503-f1" n="68" xml:id="d503-f1-content">
                <p>Not printed; Elmer Dover was Assistant Secretary of the Treasury.</p>
            </note>
        </listAnnotation>
